Sounds good — here’s a concise, practical plan to integrate an SQL-backed user account system into your existing Powerframe apps while using Firebase for user connections (auth, realtime features, push, etc.).

Recommended architecture (high level)
- Primary user store: SQL database (Postgres / MySQL) — authoritative source for user profiles, roles, billing, business logic.
- Authentication: Firebase Authentication (email/password, OAuth providers, phone) — quick, secure auth and identity lifecycle.
- Sync layer: Backend service (Powerframe app server / microservice) that links Firebase UID ↔ SQL user_id and synchronizes relevant fields.
- Real-time features / presence / messaging: Firebase Realtime Database or Firestore used for ephemeral realtime data; canonical data stays in SQL.
- Push notifications: Firebase Cloud Messaging (FCM).
- API layer: Your existing Powerframe backend exposes REST/GraphQL that reads/writes SQL, uses Firebase Admin SDK to validate tokens and perform realtime actions.

Core components & responsibilities
1. SQL user table (example fields)
   - id (primary key)
   - firebase_uid (unique, nullable until linked)
   - email, normalized_email
   - name, display_name
   - password_hash (optional if you keep SQL auth; recommended to use Firebase so not needed)
   - roles/permissions (JSON or separate roles table)
   - created_at, updated_at
   - last_login_at, picture_url, metadata (JSON)
2. Firebase Authentication
   - Sign-up / sign-in handled client-side with Firebase SDKs.
   - On first sign-in, client sends Firebase ID token to your backend.
   - Backend verifies ID token (Firebase Admin SDK) and creates or links SQL user row using firebase_uid.
3. Sync patterns
   - On create: create SQL user with firebase_uid and copy profile fields from Firebase (email, name, photo).
   - On update: use webhook (Firebase Cloud Functions or Auth triggers) or periodic sync to update SQL profile fields if you want Firebase as source-of-truth for those fields.
   - On delete: listen to Firebase Auth delete events to soft-delete SQL user or mark disabled.
4. Token validation / session flow
   - Client presents Firebase ID token to your backend on each request.
   - Backend validates token via Firebase Admin SDK, finds SQL user by firebase_uid, enforces roles/permissions from SQL.
5. Realtime & presence
   - Use Firestore/Realtime DB for presence, live connections, and ephemeral messages. Store canonical messages or long-lived data in SQL (or mirror to SQL if needed).
6. Security & rules
   - Enforce Firebase Security Rules for realtime data.
   - Use server-side role checks based on SQL roles — do not rely solely on client-provided claims.
   - Consider custom Firebase claims (set by backend) to surface roles in ID token for client-side rule use.
7. Offline / conflict handling
   - Keep user profile edits centralized (backend writes SQL, then update Firebase profile via Admin SDK) or accept Firebase-first with sync to SQL and clearly define conflict resolution.

Implementation steps (short)
1. Add firebase_uid column to your SQL users table.
2. Integrate Firebase Auth in clients for sign-up/sign-in.
3. On client sign-in, send ID token to your backend; backend verifies token, upserts SQL user with firebase_uid.
4. Use Firebase Admin SDK to set custom claims when required (roles).
5. For realtime features, implement Firestore/Realtime DB and apply Security Rules.
6. Add webhook/triggers (Cloud Functions or backend subscription) to sync deletes/updates from Firebase to SQL as needed.
7. Audit and test flows: sign-up, sign-in, profile update, role change, delete, offline sync.

Trade-offs / considerations
- Let Firebase manage credentials to reduce security burden; keep SQL for business data and authorization.
- If you need complex queries/joins across user data, keep canonical profile fields in SQL and replicate lightweight profile fields to Firebase for quick reads.
- Custom claims are limited in size; don’t store large role payloads there — use them for simple role flags.

